      "css": ".ds-nav{display:flex;gap:1.5rem;background:var(--c-canvas,#eef0ed);padding:.75rem 1rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--c-rule-soft,rgba(20,28,69,.2));font-family:'Bricolage Grotesque',ui-sans-serif,sans-serif}.ds-nav a{position:relative;display:inline-flex;align-items:center;min-height:2.5rem;font-size:.94rem;font-weight:560;color:var(--c-muted,#4c5374);text-decoration:none;transition:color 160ms ease}.ds-nav a::after{content:'';position:absolute;left:50%;bottom:.1rem;width:.4rem;height:.4rem;margin-left:-.2rem;border-radius:999px;background:var(--c-ink,#141c45);transform:scale(0);transition:transform 420ms cubic-bezier(.16,1,.3,1)}.ds-nav a:hover,.ds-nav a[aria-current=true]{color:var(--c-ink,#141c45)}.ds-nav a[aria-current=true]::after{transform:scale(1)}.ds-nav a:focus-visible{outline:2px solid var(--c-ink,#141c45);outline-offset:3px}"
    },
    {
      "name": "Status Drops",
      "kind": "chip",
      "description": "Status as a drop: filled aşı red live, ink ring in development, dotted muted archived.",
      "html": "<p class=\"ds-drops\"><span class=\"ds-drop ds-live\">Live</span><span class=\"ds-drop ds-building\">In development</span><span class=\"ds-drop ds-archived\">Archived</span></p>",
      "css": ".ds-drops{display:flex;gap:1.5rem;flex-wrap:wrap;font-family:'Bricolage Grotesque',ui-sans-serif,sans-serif}.ds-drop{display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:.5rem;font-size:.9rem;font-weight:650;line-height:1.2;white-space:nowrap}.ds-drop::before{content:'';width:.72em;height:.72em;flex:none;border-radius:999px;border:2px solid currentColor}.ds-live{color:var(--c-alarm,#a8361d)}.ds-live::before{background:currentColor}.ds-building{color:var(--c-ink,#141c45)}.ds-archived{color:var(--c-muted,#4c5374)}.ds-archived::before{border-style:dotted}"
    },
    {
      "name": "Screenshot Plate",
      "kind": "card",
      "refersTo": "plate",
      "description": "A screenshot laid on the page: paper edge only, ink edge on hover.",
      "html": "<a class=\"ds-plate\" href=\"#\"><div class=\"ds-plate-img\"></div></a>",
      "css": ".ds-plate{display:block;width:16rem;overflow:hidden;border:1px solid var(--c-rule-soft,rgba(20,28,69,.2));border-radius:.5rem;background:var(--c-raised,#e2e6e3);transition:border-color 180ms ease}.ds-plate-img{aspect-ratio:16/10;background:var(--c-raised,#e2e6e3);transition:transform 700ms cubic-bezier(.16,1,.3,1)}.ds-plate:hover,.ds-plate:focus-visible{border-color:var(--c-ink,#141c45)}.ds-plate:hover .ds-plate-img{transform:scale(1.015)}"
    },
    {
      "name": "Process Walk",
      "kind": "custom",
      "refersTo": "step-mark",
      "description": "Round step marks on a stem; reached steps fill ink, the resting step keeps an ochre ring.",
      "html": "<ol class=\"ds-steps\"><li data-now=\"true\"><span class=\"ds-mark\"></span>Idea</li><li data-now=\"true\"><span class=\"ds-mark\" data-here=\"true\"></span>Build</li><li><span class=\"ds-mark\"></span>Ship</li></ol>",
      "css": ".ds-steps{position:relative;display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(3,1fr);gap:2rem;margin:0;padding:0;list-style:none;font-family:'Bricolage Grotesque',ui-sans-serif,sans-serif;font-weight:700;color:var(--c-ink,#141c45)}.ds-steps::before{content:'';position:absolute;left:1.125rem;right:1.125rem;top:1.125rem;height:1.5px;background:var(--c-rule-soft,rgba(20,28,69,.2))}.ds-steps li{display:grid;gap:1rem}.ds-mark{position:relative;z-index:1;width:2.25rem;height:2.25rem;border-radius:999px;border:1.5px solid var(--c-ink,#141c45);background:var(--c-raised,#e2e6e3);transition:background-color 260ms ease}.ds-steps li[data-now=true] .ds-mark{background:var(--c-ink,#141c45)}.ds-mark::after{content:'';position:absolute;inset:-5px;border-radius:999px;border:2px solid var(--c-ochre,#d9a23a);transform:scale(.6);opacity:0;transition:transform 520ms cubic-bezier(.16,1,.3,1),opacity 200ms ease}.ds-mark[data-here=true]::after{transform:scale(1);opacity:1}"
    },
    {
      "name": "Motif Seal with Provenance",
      "kind": "custom",
      "description": "One numbered unit of the motif set, poured round (computed in canvas in the build; a static stand-in here) beside its provenance label.",
      "html": "<span class=\"ds-seal\"><span class=\"ds-seal-disc\"><i style=\"border-color:#d9a23a\"><i style=\"border-color:#f1f2ec\"><i style=\"border-color:#4fa39b\"><i style=\"border-color:#d98f87\"></i></i></i></i></span><span class=\"ds-prov\">No. 02 · Taraklı — computed, seed 22</span></span>",
      "css": ".ds-seal{display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:1rem;font-family:'Bricolage Grotesque',ui-sans-serif,sans-serif}.ds-seal-disc{display:grid;place-items:center;width:6rem;height:6rem;flex:none;border-radius:999px;background:var(--c-stock,#1d2a66)}.ds-seal-disc i{display:grid;place-items:center;width:calc(100% - 10px);height:calc(100% - 10px);border:4px solid;border-radius:999px}.ds-seal-disc>i{width:78%;height:78%}.ds-prov{max-width:13rem;font-size:.78rem;font-weight:650;font-stretch:78%;letter-spacing:.09em;line-height:1.4;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--c-muted,#4c5374)}"
    }
  ],
  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Ebru Tray",
    "overview": "The page is Turkish paper marbling: an idea dropped into a çivit indigo bath as pigment, combed into pattern, and pulled onto sized paper once, the same one-way trip a product makes from idea to live. Indigo carries the bath regions (the hero, the demo, the contact); cool sized paper with indigo ink carries the reading; earth pigments live only inside the marbling; and aşı red means state. Every marbled surface is computed in a canvas from a seeded polygon simulation, never an image, so the same bath is poured on every visit and every pattern can name where it came from.\n\nDensity is calm and editorial: wide bands of paper between the indigo regions, one family set large for names and small and condensed for field names, records ruled with hairlines. The drop is the one recurring shape. Status is a drop, the current nav item is a drop, the portrait sits in the innermost drop of a bülbül yuvası nest, and buttons are pulled pills whose hover is a drop spreading from the centre.\n\nThe world explicitly replaces the dark developer card grid, the warm cream editorial page, and the earlier manila case file. Nothing of the case file (Courier, manila, black stock, stamps, heavy form rules) carries over.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "A committed çivit indigo bath for hero, demo and contact; cool paper (not cream) everywhere else.",
      "Marbling is computed live from CSS-declared stones and pigments, and the stones under copy stay tone-on-tone.",
      "One family, Bricolage Grotesque, carrying every role through weight, width and optical size.",
      "Drops and pills: round marks, softly rounded sheets, no hard boxes and no shadows.",
      "Aşı red is reserved for state. Ochre is the one pigment allowed out of the bath.",
      "Motion spreads fast and settles: the pour, the pull, the walk, each played once."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The State-Only Red Rule",
        "body": "Aşı red marks live, available and active, and nothing else. If removing the red would leave the meaning intact, the red should not be there.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Ochre Leaves Once Rule",
        "body": "Ochre is the only pigment allowed outside the marbling, and only for selection, the paper button's press, and the resting process step. Turquoise, rose and lahor never appear in UI chrome.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Calm Stones Rule",
        "body": "The marbling canvas reads stones and pigments from CSS. Any region marked `data-calm` (headline, copy, actions, facts) gets tone-on-tone stones only, and bright pigment never lands under text.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The One Family Rule",
        "body": "Every role is Bricolage Grotesque. Hierarchy comes from weight, width and size, never from a second face.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Field Name Rule",
        "body": "Condensed uppercase labels name a field (Location, Facts, Stack) or a pattern's provenance. They never sit above a headline as a kicker.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Pigment-Not-Shadow Rule",
        "body": "Separation comes from a change of material (bath, paper, raised paper) or a rule, never from a cast shadow or a gradient.",
        "section": "elevation"
      }
    ],
    "dos": [
      "**Do** put the name, the demo and the contact on the Çivit Bath, and everything read at length on Sized Paper.",
      "**Do** declare marbling colours in `--bath-stones` and `--bath-pigments` per theme, and mark every text region over the bath `data-calm`.",
      "**Do** compute every marbled surface from a seed with a numbered motif (01 Gelgit, 02 Taraklı, 03 Battal) and print its provenance beside it.",
      "**Do** mark status with a drop: filled aşı red for live or available, an ink ring for in development, a dotted muted ring for archived.",
      "**Do** use the centre-out spread (520ms, spread ease) for pill hovers, and the spread ease for every authored transition.",
      "**Do** keep text pairs at WCAG AA or better in both themes, including bath-alarm over the lightest stone."
    ],
    "donts": [
      "**Don't** use aşı red as a pigment, an accent or decoration, and don't use rose where red is meant.",
      "**Don't** let turquoise, rose or lahor into UI chrome. Ochre is the only pigment that leaves the bath, and only for selection, the paper button's press and the resting step.",
      "**Don't** use a raster image for marbling. Patterns are computed.",
      "**Don't** introduce a second typeface, a cream or warm paper ground, or a dark card grid.",
      "**Don't** add box shadows, CSS gradients or hard rectangular cards to UI. Depth is material and rules. The page curl's simulated fold shading is the only exception.",
      "**Don't** put a condensed label above a headline as a kicker or eyebrow.",
      "**Don't** add looping or drifting motion, or per-section entrance animations beyond the strip pull and the process walk."
    ]
  }
}